To mitigate this cost, recent work turns to approximate attention. Importance hashing and locality-sensitive sampling reduce the expected number of scored edges from $\Theta(d)$ to $\Theta(\log d)$ \cite{hua-2022-transformer}. HiPPO-GAT adopts the same principle for graphs, yet still performs an $O(\log d)$ search at every epoch and rescans the same neighbours thousands of times on hubs. Measurements on OGB-Products reveal that after only three training epochs more than $90\%$ of a nodes top-32 attentive neighbours remain unchanged. Temporal redundancy, not spatial search, has therefore become the dominant obstacle to further complexity reductions.  
